Before it sleeps, handles the X11 events xcb read while a request waited.

Backported from upstream's 779151fdfc ("fix: drain X11 events buffered
during Wayland dispatch"), which no release carries yet, and made on
flush-when-full.patch, which applies before it.

xcb reads the events that come in while a synchronous request waits for
its reply, and queues them. Satellite's main loop handles X11 events,
then makes such requests while it dispatches Wayland and sets a new
clipboard (interning a mime's atoms, for one), then polls the X11
socket. An event read into xcb's queue meanwhile leaves the socket with
nothing to read, so poll sleeps over it until another event comes. An
X11 window can then go unseen, with no Wayland surface, until its
program does something else or quits. In core.nix's
vm-desktop-keeps-copies, 6 of 62 runs opened xlogo and saw no window
for a minute, and in each the satellite's log met xlogo's window only
as the test killed it, its pid already gone. The main loop now handles
whatever xcb queued before it polls, and goes round again when there
was any. Going round starves no Wayland work: the loop's top dispatches
Xwayland's requests, then server_state.run() reads the compositor's
socket, polling it with no wait, and handle_clientside_events() at its
end flushes what is queued for the compositor, a flush left pending
included. So a steady stream of X11 events delays Wayland only as long
as each turn takes.

